   "title": "How to use · v1.1.0",
   "widgets_values": [
    "## How to use · v1.1.0\n\n**What it does:** edits one photo with FLUX 3 Image Klein (distilled, int8) for 6 to 12 GB cards: no prompt upsampler, three files. Core ComfyUI nodes only.\n\n1. **Your inputs** (green group):\n   - **Photo to edit**: one picture. The result keeps its shape.\n   - **What to change**: say the change, then say what must stay (\"Keep the woman, the blue background ... unchanged\"). There is no upsampler here, so write the full sentence yourself.\n   - **Output megapixels**: 1 = about 1024x1024, the size every test run used (1360x768 for a 16:9 photo).\n   - **Seed**: a new one every run. Set \"control after generate\" to fixed to keep a picture.\n2. **Models**: three files from the Downloads note. Nothing to change.\n3. **Prompt + photo**: nothing to change. The photo goes in as the reference and also sets the output size.\n4. **Sampling**: 16 steps, CFG 1, euler / simple, shift 2.77: BFL's template values.\n5. Press **Run**. The picture is saved to `output/Flux3Klein/`.\n\n**What to expect:** about 15 to 17 s per picture at 1360x768 (1 MP) on the lab card, and the same time inside a 6, 8 or 12 GiB budget. A real 6 or 8 GB card is slower: not measured.\n\n**Why no upsampler:** BFL's upsampler is a 6.7 GB extra file. Leaving it out means 11.0 GB of downloads instead of 17.7 GB. You write the long instruction yourself. The other two files in this folder still have the upsampler.\n\n**Tested on:** ComfyUI 0.45.3 on an RTX PRO 4000 Blackwell 24 GB, capped to 6, 8 and 12 GiB budgets with --reserve-vram, 64 GB RAM. Not run on a real 6, 8 or 12 GB card.\n"
   ]
  },
